\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Sprinkling and dimension for a causal set}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{A causal set is a locally finite partial order
(\citet{Phys. Rev. Lett. 59 (1987) 522}{bombelli1987prl}).
A faithful embedding into a time-oriented Lorentzian manifold
asks for three conditions: the induced causal relations agree
with the order, the image points have unit density, and the
continuum geometry varies on a scale much larger than the mean
spacing. The letter expects such an embedding, when one exists,
to be unique up to an approximate isometry that preserves the
causal set. Its argument that a small Alexandrov neighborhood
determines the dimension is a sketch, and the letter defers the
proof.}

\p{The same letter describes the image as a random lattice made
by sprinkling points. In an Alexandrov neighborhood the number
of points equals the volume, up to the Poisson fluctuations of
that sprinkling. Selecting each element independently with one
fixed probability is the coarse graining defined there. The
dimension read after that selection need not equal the
dimension read before it.}

\p{A Poisson process of unit intensity on a globally hyperbolic
Lorentzian manifold induces a causal set by the order of the
manifold (\citet{arXiv:gr-qc/0309009v1}{sorkin2003causalsets}).
A regular lattice in two-dimensional Minkowski space, with
integer null coordinates, fails to stay uniform under a boost,
and the exposition uses the Poisson process for that reason.
The exposition states, as a conjecture, that two manifolds
which both approximate one causal set are approximately
isometric. For an interval that approximates an interval in
Minkowski space, a midpoint is an element that maximizes the
smaller of the two subintervals it determines, and
#{\log_2(N/N')} estimates the dimension. Here #{N} is the
number of elements in the interval and #{N'} is the number in
that smaller subinterval. The same exposition gives an
estimator from the number of relations, and it says that these
flat-space estimates are not yet shown for a generic curved
metric. None of these statements identifies a cycle, a
hierarchical precision, or a gasket graph with a causal set.}
